#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""kit: build the transfer for an air-gapped environment from kit.yml, then load it inside.

On the connected workstation (WSL or Linux, with internet access):

  python3 kit check      check kit.yml, the tools, and that every source can be reached
  python3 kit fetch      download and verify everything into one transfer folder
  python3 kit catalog    what broadcom/catalog.yml offers, and what each environment turns on
  python3 kit choose --env ENV
                         pick what that environment gets from the catalog (before a fetch)
  python3 kit catalog --refresh
                         look for newer versions in Broadcom's registry and ask yes or no
                         before each change (--yes for unattended runs, --dry-run to only look)

Inside the environment, from the repository the transfer carried in:

  python3 kit publish --from DIR --env ENV   check the transfer and stage it (and upload the
                                             Harbor files to Nexus, when the environment uses one)
  python3 kit setup   --env ENV              answer a few questions; the inventory, certificate
                                             and vault are written for you (a new environment
                                             starts from the template; run it again to change any)
  python3 kit build   --env ENV              check everything, then build or update the registry
  python3 kit load    --env ENV              push the Broadcom items that environment turns on
                                             into Harbor, and write the service YAMLs for it
  python3 kit verify  --env ENV              check the registry; --from DIR checks a transfer
  python3 kit doctor  --env ENV              everything build checks, without building
  python3 kit remove  --env ENV              take Harbor off the server; its images stay

Needs python3 (3.6 or later), bash and curl. cosign and imgpkg are installed into
~/.local/bin when missing, each checked against its release checksums. Nothing
needs sudo, except creating the staging directory inside the environment.

Exit codes: 0 done, 1 a usage or environment problem, 2 something failed its check.
"""

